WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.000
Okay, so we've learned how to create an app.

00:02.000 --> 00:04.000
We've attached some reactive data. That was fun,

00:04.000 --> 00:05.000
hopefully pretty easy to do.

00:05.000 --> 00:07.000
And now, of course, we want to talk about

00:07.000 --> 00:08.000
how we deal with user input, right?

00:08.000 --> 00:10.000
Because that's what a lot of JavaScript's value is,

00:10.000 --> 00:13.000
is handling the interactivity of your website.

00:13.000 --> 00:15.000
So the first thing we want to talk about

00:15.000 --> 00:18.000
is let's open Playground actually in Chrome.

00:18.000 --> 00:19.000
So we have that.

00:19.000 --> 00:23.000
So let me just open a new tab with this,

00:23.000 --> 00:26.000
and this will be Playground.

00:26.000 --> 00:30.000
Okay, so for the sake of Playground demo purposes,

00:30.000 --> 00:34.000
let's just use the standard example of the counter app, right?

00:34.000 --> 00:37.000
Which is the fact that if we go inside of here,

00:37.000 --> 00:40.000
and this time I'm going to have, let's see, let's do that sequentially.

00:40.000 --> 00:43.000
So I'll put a horizontal line here, line break, there we go.

00:43.000 --> 00:47.000
And then here we'll just do an h1, we'll call it counter.

00:47.000 --> 00:48.000
We have a count, and then we have a button

00:48.000 --> 00:51.000
where we want to increment count.

00:51.000 --> 00:55.000
This should look pretty familiar, 10.

00:55.000 --> 00:58.000
Okay, so if you scroll down,

00:58.000 --> 01:01.000
ooh, I don't like the fact that this is all the way at the bottom.

01:01.000 --> 01:04.000
I'm going to actually move this up to the top.

01:04.000 --> 01:08.000
Copy that, hr, great.

01:08.000 --> 01:10.000
So we'll do this from most recent to oldest.

01:10.000 --> 01:13.000
Okay, so here we have our counter,

01:13.000 --> 01:14.000
and your question is probably thinking,

01:14.000 --> 01:17.000
okay, how do we actually change data?

01:17.000 --> 01:20.000
Because after all, it's fun to render some reactive stuff,

01:20.000 --> 01:21.000
we want to do more with it.

01:21.000 --> 01:24.000
So inside of view, we have our data here,

01:24.000 --> 01:27.000
but the question is we want to actually change it.

01:27.000 --> 01:29.000
And so the option that we want to learn about next

01:29.000 --> 01:32.000
is the concept of methods.

01:32.000 --> 01:34.000
And so if we think about functions in JavaScript,

01:34.000 --> 01:38.000
right, typically we would have something like let count equals zero,

01:38.000 --> 01:41.000
and then you'd have a function called increment,

01:41.000 --> 01:45.000
and it would say count++, something along these lines.

01:45.000 --> 01:48.000
When you think of methods, think of it as functions from JavaScript

01:48.000 --> 01:50.000
that are available for your app to call at any time.

01:50.000 --> 01:52.000
That's the best way to think about it.

01:52.000 --> 01:55.000
So from this perspective, we would actually want to then define a method.

01:55.000 --> 01:59.000
We'll just say increment count.

01:59.000 --> 02:05.000
And inside of here, this is where a question that we had earlier comes into play.

02:05.000 --> 02:08.000
So when you want to refer to something else in the app.

02:08.000 --> 02:13.000
So in this case, we want to actually refer to this count property inside of data.

02:13.000 --> 02:19.000
View is smart about a lot of things, but if we just currently say count++,

02:19.000 --> 02:21.000
this is not going to work.

02:21.000 --> 02:24.000
And the reason for that is because if we know when it comes to JavaScript

02:24.000 --> 02:26.000
and programming, scoping is a thing.

02:26.000 --> 02:29.000
So it doesn't know basically where to fetch count from.

02:29.000 --> 02:34.000
And so this is where the dreaded keyword of this comes into play

02:34.000 --> 02:35.000
when it comes to JavaScript.

02:35.000 --> 02:39.000
The this keyword is where view basically takes the context of the app,

02:39.000 --> 02:42.000
and it will basically figure out where count exists.

02:42.000 --> 02:46.000
So from view 2, you might have noticed it from like this.data.count.

02:46.000 --> 02:48.000
This would be an explicit thing you might do.

02:48.000 --> 02:52.000
But because view knows that oftentimes we're referring to data or compute it,

02:52.000 --> 02:55.000
it will, unless you have some really clear name clashes,

02:55.000 --> 02:58.000
you can just use this.whatever property you're looking for.

02:58.000 --> 03:02.000
And that goes for basically anything you're defining within this options API.

03:02.000 --> 03:08.000
And the benefit of this is that while some people find that this keyword confusing,

03:08.000 --> 03:11.000
the point of it, I think at the time when the API was being designed,

03:11.000 --> 03:14.000
is around the fact that you shouldn't really have to think about what this even means.

03:14.000 --> 03:16.000
The point is that I think of this as the app,

03:16.000 --> 03:19.000
and that way you come in and you refer to this app, and that's it.

03:19.000 --> 03:23.000
So when I was teaching other people view for the first time,

03:23.000 --> 03:25.000
even though they didn't have a lot of JavaScript experience,

03:25.000 --> 03:28.000
they got the convention that as long as I call this, it refers to my app,

03:28.000 --> 03:30.000
and I'm not going to learn about scoping or anything.

03:30.000 --> 03:31.000
It just works.

03:31.000 --> 03:34.000
And that in itself is an advantage from a learning perspective.

03:34.000 --> 03:37.000
But just know that you don't have to use this

03:37.000 --> 03:40.000
so that in the future if people are actively avoiding it,

03:40.000 --> 03:43.000
we can go ahead and do that once we learn about Composition API.

03:43.000 --> 03:45.000
But while we're in the Options API right now,

03:45.000 --> 03:49.000
this is how we actually refer to things within this configuration object.

03:49.000 --> 03:50.000
Okay.

03:50.000 --> 03:54.000
So now that we have the thisCount++, what we're going to do here now

03:54.000 --> 03:57.000
is we're going to go and say, how do we actually call the thing?

03:57.000 --> 03:59.000
And so what we're going to do is we're going to,

03:59.000 --> 04:03.000
just like before, we're going to use a view directive to actually call the thing.

04:03.000 --> 04:05.000
And so we're going to use v-on.

04:05.000 --> 04:07.000
That is the directive that you'll want to learn for this

04:07.000 --> 04:10.000
because similar to what you know from JavaScript events,

04:10.000 --> 04:14.000
onClick, onKeyUp, this is the same idea.

04:14.000 --> 04:17.000
We're extending on that theory.

04:17.000 --> 04:19.000
And so instead of just like onClick, though,

04:19.000 --> 04:22.000
which you might think that's what I'm probably going to guess,

04:22.000 --> 04:26.000
view, because v-on itself is the directive,

04:26.000 --> 04:29.000
you have the ability to pass it an argument through the colon symbol.

04:29.000 --> 04:32.000
So it's v-on colon the event that you want to call.

04:32.000 --> 04:35.000
And the event you want to call, again, is standard JavaScript events.

04:35.000 --> 04:36.000
We haven't made anything up.

04:36.000 --> 04:41.000
So key up anything that you can think of that's standard in the JavaScript API,

04:41.000 --> 04:42.000
that exists.

04:42.000 --> 04:45.000
And so when you click, what do we want to refer to?

04:45.000 --> 04:46.000
Well, guess what?

04:46.000 --> 04:50.000
We want to refer to our increment count function that we had earlier.

04:50.000 --> 04:52.000
The key thing here to remember, though,

04:52.000 --> 04:54.000
is that you don't want to actually call the function

04:54.000 --> 04:57.000
because this is something that will be called when the event is triggered.

04:57.000 --> 05:01.000
So basically you're passing a reference to the function inside of your app,

05:01.000 --> 05:03.000
and then when it's clicked, it will be called.

05:03.000 --> 05:05.000
That's the reason why you don't call it immediately.

05:05.000 --> 05:10.000
So if we save that, and let's go ahead and now refresh our page,

05:10.000 --> 05:13.000
you can see, boom, things are actually changing.

05:13.000 --> 05:16.000
And they're being tracked.

05:16.000 --> 05:18.000
So that's fairly straightforward.

05:18.000 --> 05:20.000
What about something a little bit more complicated,

05:20.000 --> 05:22.000
like what if I wanted to set the amount

05:22.000 --> 05:24.000
that the counter is being incremented by?

05:24.000 --> 05:26.000
Okay, well, in this case, you're going to say,

05:26.000 --> 05:28.000
well, let's say we have a label here.

05:28.000 --> 05:31.000
Let's say increment amount.

05:31.000 --> 05:34.000
And then it'll be increment by.

05:34.000 --> 05:39.000
And then you probably have some element here that's a number.

05:39.000 --> 05:41.000
And then so if we refresh this, theoretically,

05:41.000 --> 05:44.000
what you would want is the ability to change the amount

05:44.000 --> 05:46.000
that increment count is changing.

05:46.000 --> 05:48.000
So how would we actually do that?

05:48.000 --> 05:50.000
Well, first of all, what we want to do

05:50.000 --> 05:52.000
is we need a reactive data property

05:52.000 --> 05:54.000
because we want to track what the heck is going on.

05:54.000 --> 05:57.000
So at this moment, if we go down into our data,

05:57.000 --> 05:59.000
we have this count, and we can say,

05:59.000 --> 06:02.000
we can just have increment amount be a reactive data property.

06:02.000 --> 06:05.000
And currently, it's one.

06:05.000 --> 06:09.000
And then what we want to do then is tie it into our increment count down here.

06:09.000 --> 06:11.000
Let me actually collapse this.

06:11.000 --> 06:15.000
And so rather than just incrementing it by one, which is the plus plus,

06:15.000 --> 06:19.000
we'll do plus equals this.increment amount.

06:19.000 --> 06:28.000
And to show that this is working, I'm going to switch this over to 10.

06:28.000 --> 06:29.000
And we're going to go ahead and refresh this.

06:29.000 --> 06:32.000
And you can see it's jumping, as you would expect, by 10.

06:32.000 --> 06:35.000
But we want to be able to change this, right?

06:35.000 --> 06:38.000
How do we actually basically connect these two?

06:38.000 --> 06:41.000
So let me go ahead and actually wrap this part in the div real quick.

06:41.000 --> 06:44.000
Oops, that's not what I wanted at all.

06:44.000 --> 06:47.000
Da, da, da, div.

06:47.000 --> 06:48.000
There we go.

06:48.000 --> 06:49.000
Okay.

06:49.000 --> 06:50.000
That should give it a new line.

06:50.000 --> 06:51.000
Perfect.

06:51.000 --> 06:52.000
Okay.

06:52.000 --> 06:57.000
So the first thing first is we need our input to display what it is that value is.

06:57.000 --> 06:58.000
So how do we do that?

06:58.000 --> 07:00.000
Normally, we would say value equals 10.

07:00.000 --> 07:02.000
This is standard HTML.

07:02.000 --> 07:04.000
But this is static, right?

07:04.000 --> 07:07.000
This is something we define ourselves, has nothing to do with our app.

07:07.000 --> 07:08.000
How do we connect the two?

07:08.000 --> 07:11.000
The way we do that is with another view directive.

07:11.000 --> 07:12.000
Because what are we doing?

07:12.000 --> 07:15.000
We're now extending upon our HTML to give it super power to do what?

07:15.000 --> 07:19.000
To have some interactivity, to be supercharged with reactive data.

07:19.000 --> 07:22.000
And so you do that via vbind, right?

07:22.000 --> 07:24.000
You're binding the value to a dynamic property.

07:24.000 --> 07:25.000
That's the way to think about it.

07:25.000 --> 07:27.000
But here, though, what are we binding it to?

07:27.000 --> 07:28.000
We're not binding it to 10.

07:28.000 --> 07:29.000
That's not what we're binding by.

07:29.000 --> 07:31.000
We're binding it to increment amount.

07:31.000 --> 07:35.000
So we can refer directly to the variable that we talked about earlier.

07:35.000 --> 07:41.000
And so to show that this changed, this time I'm going to change increment amount to 8.

07:41.000 --> 07:43.000
And if I refresh, boom, there we go.

07:43.000 --> 07:46.000
We can see that they're now talking to each other.

07:46.000 --> 07:49.000
And so now we can see when we increment count, this is great.

07:49.000 --> 07:50.000
It's actually changing.

07:50.000 --> 07:52.000
But what do we know about this?

07:52.000 --> 07:56.000
We know that if we change now to 12, is it going to make a difference?

07:56.000 --> 07:57.000
No, it's not.

07:57.000 --> 07:59.000
In fact, it's going to reset itself back to 8.

07:59.000 --> 08:00.000
What's going on?

08:00.000 --> 08:04.000
Well, the reason for this is because you've told Vue, track this thing and make sure that

08:04.000 --> 08:07.000
its value is always bound to this increment amount.

08:07.000 --> 08:09.000
That's what you've told it.

08:09.000 --> 08:12.000
And so this is why even though we've changed it once and we try to increment the count,

08:12.000 --> 08:17.000
it's setting it back because that's what Vue is doing its job that you told it to do.

08:17.000 --> 08:18.000
So what do we want to do?

08:18.000 --> 08:23.000
We want to actually update increment amount whenever someone actually types inside of the input element.

08:23.000 --> 08:24.000
So how might you do that?

08:24.000 --> 08:27.000
Well, we're going to actually listen to an event.

08:27.000 --> 08:28.000
And so what is that?

08:28.000 --> 08:32.000
We learned it is V on, and we're going to listen to the input event.

08:32.000 --> 08:36.000
And what we're not going to do, though, is we're not going to inline the function that will change this

08:36.000 --> 08:39.000
because I find that to be very clunky.

08:39.000 --> 08:41.000
So we're going to call it change increment amount.

08:41.000 --> 08:47.000
And so what we're doing here is we're saying, hey, on the input event for this input element,

08:47.000 --> 08:52.000
when we get an input event, I want you to run this function, which we have not created yet, to be clear.

08:52.000 --> 09:00.000
So we'll come in here, and we'll go to a method, and we'll say change increment amount.

09:00.000 --> 09:02.000
Just like that.

09:02.000 --> 09:04.000
And here's the difference, though.

09:04.000 --> 09:08.000
Now that you're actually listening to an event, you actually get a default event object

09:08.000 --> 09:12.000
that's passed to you, once again, just like you would expect from standard JavaScript.

09:12.000 --> 09:13.000
So I'm going to call that event.

09:13.000 --> 09:17.000
And what we're going to do is, though, we're just going to log the event to prove that this is working.

09:17.000 --> 09:19.000
So I'm going to go ahead and open this up, refresh.

09:19.000 --> 09:25.000
And if I go ahead and type in here, anything happening, look, the input event is being fired.

09:25.000 --> 09:26.000
So what do we know?

09:26.000 --> 09:34.000
Well, we know, again, we won't dive into the details of it, but event.target.value is what you're looking for.

09:34.000 --> 09:38.000
That's usually the convention for when you're playing with form elements.

09:38.000 --> 09:43.000
So to show you this works, if I delete this, you can see it's actually showing up inside of my terminal,

09:43.000 --> 09:45.000
as you would expect.

09:45.000 --> 09:49.000
And so now that we're actually getting the dynamic value of the event,

09:49.000 --> 09:58.000
we can then say this.increment amount is equal to event.target.value.

09:58.000 --> 10:01.000
Now, to show that this works, we're just going to also,

10:01.000 --> 10:04.000
I'm a big fan of, like, making sure things are actually working correctly.

10:04.000 --> 10:12.000
So I'm going to go ahead and inside of here, we're just going to add a quick h1 for the increment amount.

10:12.000 --> 10:15.000
So you can see here, we're rendering the increment amount here and here.

10:15.000 --> 10:19.000
And if I change this now to 18, you'll see that it is being tracked correctly.

10:19.000 --> 10:20.000
It's in here.

10:20.000 --> 10:25.000
And now we can increment the count, and then, hmm, it's not working as correctly.

10:25.000 --> 10:26.000
Anybody have a guess why?

10:26.000 --> 10:27.000
String.

10:27.000 --> 10:28.000
It's a string.

10:28.000 --> 10:29.000
It's a string!

10:29.000 --> 10:30.000
That's right.

10:30.000 --> 10:32.000
So you might think, okay, well, you know what I'm going to do?

10:32.000 --> 10:36.000
I'm going to come in here, I'm going to wrap this in a number, be done with it.

10:36.000 --> 10:39.000
All right, let's refresh, let's make sure that works.

10:39.000 --> 10:41.000
Let's do 80 this time.

10:41.000 --> 10:42.000
All right, that works.

10:42.000 --> 10:44.000
But I'll do you one better.

10:44.000 --> 10:48.000
Instead of having to come in here and do it here, which is kind of silly,

10:48.000 --> 10:51.000
because the increment amount, it already has a target value.

10:51.000 --> 10:55.000
And what's a bit odd, too, is that, huh, if we look at our input element,

10:55.000 --> 10:57.000
we are already typed number.

10:57.000 --> 10:58.000
Why is that the case?

10:58.000 --> 11:01.000
Well, this is one way to fix it.

11:01.000 --> 11:03.000
Here's the thing, though.

11:03.000 --> 11:08.000
If we take a look at our inputs, Sam had made the joke that I showed in the tweet

11:08.000 --> 11:12.000
earlier that most of the web is rendering a list.

11:12.000 --> 11:15.000
Well, I'd say a lot of the web is also filling out forms, right?

11:15.000 --> 11:18.000
I mean, that's what a lot of our bread and butter is, too.

11:18.000 --> 11:22.000
So Vue was like, okay, this is a lot of work to do every single time you have a form

11:22.000 --> 11:23.000
element, right?

11:23.000 --> 11:26.000
Like, oh, gosh, what if we could just learn from what Angular did

11:26.000 --> 11:29.000
and do two-way binding in this particular context?

11:29.000 --> 11:34.000
So instead of having to always define and change and track all this stuff,

11:34.000 --> 11:37.000
what if we had a directive called vModel,

11:37.000 --> 11:41.000
and we tracked it directly to the data property that we cared about?

11:43.000 --> 11:48.000
Once you have this vModel, all of this, I say disappears,

11:48.000 --> 11:53.000
but vModel is syntactic sugar for basically configuring all that underneath the hood,

11:53.000 --> 11:58.000
which means that inside of our method down here, we don't need even the this as well.

11:58.000 --> 12:00.000
So I can save that.

12:00.000 --> 12:04.000
Now if we refresh, once again, let's do 800 this time and increment,

12:04.000 --> 12:07.000
you'll see that things are changing just as you would expect with this vModel.

12:07.000 --> 12:13.000
And what's even more interesting, if you'll notice, it's not a string anymore.

12:13.000 --> 12:18.000
Because once again, what Vue tries to do is it looks at the developer experience

12:18.000 --> 12:22.000
and it goes, hey, I know that I'm looking at an input element.

12:22.000 --> 12:25.000
Because here's the thing, vModel works with other form elements like checkboxes,

12:25.000 --> 12:26.000
radio, select.

12:26.000 --> 12:27.000
You can do all that.

12:27.000 --> 12:28.000
You can do all that.

12:28.000 --> 12:32.000
But it also knows what kind of input event to listen for.

12:32.000 --> 12:34.000
Because you'll know that, especially with select, that one's a tricky one.

12:34.000 --> 12:36.000
I always have to look up the docs every time I work with select.

12:36.000 --> 12:39.000
I have to code it from scratch.

12:39.000 --> 12:43.000
But here, it actually knows that we are a tight number in here.

12:43.000 --> 12:47.000
And because of that, the value is actually tracking inside of its app

12:47.000 --> 12:49.000
has automatically been converted.

12:49.000 --> 12:53.000
Now if we did string, for example, here, or not string, text, revert,

12:53.000 --> 12:59.000
you can see, oh, it actually is smart enough to even know what to do.

12:59.000 --> 13:05.000
But the one thing I wanted to show, because this used to be a Vue2 thing,

13:05.000 --> 13:11.000
is that you could actually attach arguments or basically modifiers to your directives.

13:11.000 --> 13:16.000
So in this case, it's a vModel of number, for example.

13:16.000 --> 13:18.000
But there will be other opportunities.

13:18.000 --> 13:20.000
I'll get to present that later on.

